WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:02.834
-Welcome back to "Late Night".
We're here with Billy Porter.

00:00:02.834 --> 00:00:05.200
You went on a 25 city tour?
-Yes.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.501
-And how did you feel?

00:00:06.501 --> 00:00:09.000
What was your experience
traveling the country?

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.968
-It was wonderful
to be back as myself.

00:00:12.968 --> 00:00:15.634
You know, I've become famous
playing characters,

00:00:15.634 --> 00:00:17.434
so it was nice
to be back as myself.

00:00:17.434 --> 00:00:18.901
It was also very nice --

00:00:18.901 --> 00:00:20.834
You know, Brandi Carlile
talks about this.

00:00:20.834 --> 00:00:24.501
She travels all over the world,
and the people that she meets,

00:00:24.501 --> 00:00:28.234
you know, she -- she realizes
that America is not as divided

00:00:28.234 --> 00:00:30.767
as our news cycles
might make us think.

00:00:30.767 --> 00:00:34.033
And that was so --
it was so special

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:38.567
to just go out and touch people
and reach people and hug people

00:00:38.567 --> 00:00:40.300
and give the world
a big bear hug.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:43.067
And just, like, you know,
I just wanted to bring joy

00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:44.834
and hope again and peace again

00:00:44.834 --> 00:00:47.367
and, you know, make people
talk to each other again.

00:00:47.367 --> 00:00:49.734
You know, we've been through
this collective trauma

00:00:49.734 --> 00:00:52.968
where we've, you know,
where we've separated,

00:00:52.968 --> 00:00:54.901
and it's time to sort of
come back together.

00:00:54.901 --> 00:00:57.934
And so, the -- the concert
really did that for me.

00:00:57.934 --> 00:01:01.300
It was 25 cities
and five weeks,

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.667
and people came,
which was fabulous.

00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:08.567
And, you know,
audiences from age 9 to 90,

00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:13.033
all races and colors and creeds
and sexualities and genders,

00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:15.067
it was -- it was magical.

00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:17.067
It was amazing, and I can't wait

00:01:17.067 --> 00:01:18.567
to go back out.
-That's exciting.

00:01:18.567 --> 00:01:21.067
So, that's a lot of shows
in a very short period of time.

00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:23.033
Did your energy ever flag,
or did you just ride

00:01:23.033 --> 00:01:24.934
that enthusiasm of the audience?
-Well, I've been doing

00:01:24.934 --> 00:01:26.601
eight shows a week
for over 30 years.

00:01:26.601 --> 00:01:28.133
That's my wheelhouse.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:28.133 --> 00:01:29.367
-You know,
everybody was concerned.

00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:31.100
I'm like,
"That's not the problem."

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:32.767
-[ Laughs ]
-The problem ain't me

00:01:32.767 --> 00:01:35.601
doing the show, the problem
is my budget ain't big enough.

00:01:35.601 --> 00:01:37.234
[ Laughter ]

00:01:37.234 --> 00:01:40.133
Don't worry about the show.
I got that part.

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:41.801
-[ Laughing ]
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:44.067
-You mentioned writing
all the songs in your album.

00:01:44.067 --> 00:01:46.801
What is your creative process?
Are you collaborative?

00:01:46.801 --> 00:01:49.234
Do you do it fully on your own?
-No, no, no, no, no, no.

00:01:49.234 --> 00:01:51.000
I can't do it on my own.
I come from the theater.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.667
It's a very collaborative
art form.

00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:55.934
You know, I write scripts,
I write plays,

00:01:55.934 --> 00:01:57.400
I do some of that by myself.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.133
I also have a writing partner
for that.

00:02:00.133 --> 00:02:02.868
But with the music,
I don't play any instruments.

00:02:02.868 --> 00:02:06.033
So I need a crew.

00:02:06.033 --> 00:02:07.767
And this time, it was great

00:02:07.767 --> 00:02:11.267
because unlike
my first experience,

00:02:11.267 --> 00:02:13.267
these people were actually
listening to me.

00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:15.000
-Right.
-And listening to what it was

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.868
that I want to do --
wanted to do.

00:02:16.868 --> 00:02:21.267
It was Justin Tranter, the late,
great Andrea Martin,

00:02:21.267 --> 00:02:23.901
Emily K. and Jade Thirlwall
over in London,

00:02:23.901 --> 00:02:26.667
and, you know, we would just
sit, and we would talk,

00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:28.834
and then, we would just
build and layer.

00:02:28.834 --> 00:02:31.133
I'm a -- I'm an ideas guy,

00:02:31.133 --> 00:02:34.767
I'm a lyric guy,
I'm a top line melody guy.

00:02:34.767 --> 00:02:37.968
And then, all the other stuff,
you know, falls into place.

00:02:37.968 --> 00:02:40.901
It was really great.
I had such a great time.

00:02:40.901 --> 00:02:45.701
And these artisans really
helped me with my vision,

00:02:45.701 --> 00:02:47.567
and helped me realize

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:52.434
this really specific
and individual vision.

00:02:52.434 --> 00:02:53.868
-Were you there
for the whole process?

00:02:53.868 --> 00:02:55.400
Was there ever a time
where you, like, went home

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:56.934
at the end of the night,
and when you came back,

00:02:56.934 --> 00:02:58.767
they'd done work on it
and it felt like,

00:02:58.767 --> 00:03:00.701
you know, the -- the elves
who do the cobbling?

00:03:00.701 --> 00:03:03.033
-Yes.
You know, the production part.

00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:05.133
-Yeah.
-That happens in the production.

00:03:05.133 --> 00:03:06.501
-And that's so amazing.
-Yes.

00:03:06.501 --> 00:03:08.501
-I mean, the few times I --
It's not for music,

00:03:08.501 --> 00:03:10.167
but it's, you know,
you see an edit of something,

00:03:10.167 --> 00:03:11.667
you see graphics
that have been added,

00:03:11.667 --> 00:03:15.000
and it is so stunning to see
what these people can do.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.234
-It's gorgeous. And you know,
I can't do everything...

00:03:19.234 --> 00:03:20.300
by myself.
-But you come pretty close.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:21.701
You come pretty close.
-I come close,

00:03:21.701 --> 00:03:24.133
but I can't do everything
by myself.

00:03:24.133 --> 00:03:26.000
And I-I don't know.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.234
I like the collaboration process
so much.

00:03:29.234 --> 00:03:30.767
I really, really do.

00:03:30.767 --> 00:03:32.901
You know, people are
in the room with you.

00:03:32.901 --> 00:03:35.267
I find writing sometimes
to be isolating --

00:03:35.267 --> 00:03:36.767
-Yeah.
-...when you're alone

00:03:36.767 --> 00:03:38.534
and by yourself, and, you know.

00:03:38.534 --> 00:03:41.767
So I'm a people person,
and I love to be with my people.

00:03:41.767 --> 00:03:44.133
-Well, we're very --
We love to have you with us.

00:03:44.133 --> 00:03:46.100
And we thank you so much
for making the time for us.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:48.567
Congratulations on the album.

00:03:48.567 --> 00:03:51.400
-"Black Mona Lisa",
November 17th.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.801
Stick around. We'll be right
back with Darren Aronofsky.

